  <span class="mono-label">RING 1 · CORNERSTONE</span>
  <h1>What makes fresh graduates unhireable and how do you fix it?</h1>
  <p class="answer-capsule">Fresh graduates are unhireable when they have zero proof of output: no artifact a recruiter can open, inspect, and forward to a hiring manager. A scored work sample that demonstrates actual capability before anyone asks for a transcript is the most reliable way to bridge that gap.</p>

  <div class="tldr">
    <ul>
      <li><a href="https://www.shrm.org/topics-tools/news/talent-acquisition/skills-gap-college-graduates" target="_blank">72% of employers say new graduates lack practical skills</a>, per 2024 SHRM data.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.apa.org/pubs/journals/features/bul-124-2-262.pdf" target="_blank">Work-sample tests predict job performance better than unstructured interviews</a>, according to Schmidt and Hunter's landmark validity research, confirmed by <a href="https://psycnet.apa.org/record/2023-38400-001" target="_blank">Sackett et al. 2022 meta-analysis in Personnel Psychology</a>.</li>
      <li>A Middle East university pilot, Spring 2026, showed per-submission feedback drove repeat engagement over generic career advice.</li>
      <li>Most recruiters already have 200 applications per junior role. A scored work sample is the thing that makes them open yours rather than someone else's, and the difference shows up in who gets called back first.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <section>
    <h2>Why do recruiters say fresh graduates are unhireable?</h2>
    <p>A single junior role pulls <a href="https://www.jobvite.com/resources/blog/recruiting-metrics-benchmark-report/" target="_blank">between 100 and 300 applications on average</a>, and the first cut is measured in seconds, not minutes. A <a href="https://www.theladders.com/static/images/basicSite/pdfs/TheLadders-EyeTracking-StudyC2.pdf" target="_blank">2018 eye-tracking study by TheLadders</a> clocked the median résumé review at roughly 7.4 seconds. University name, last job title, maybe a company logo if the formatting makes it easy to spot. That is the full evaluation you get before someone decides whether to scroll further or hit the archive button.</p>
    <p>A degree from a name-brand school gets you into the maybe pile, and once you are there, the credential has done its job and has nothing left to offer.</p>
    <p>The trouble really surfaces during screening interviews, where graduates lean on coursework or group projects that split credit four ways with no way to isolate individual contribution. There is nothing the recruiter can click on, download, or forward to a hiring manager. SHRM labelled this the <a href="https://www.shrm.org/topics-tools/news/talent-acquisition/skills-gap-college-graduates" target="_blank">skills gap</a>, and their 2024 survey puts it at 72% of HR professionals saying entry-level candidates lack applied abilities. That number has held steady through three survey cycles now, so it is not a blip.</p>
    <p>I have sat through campus recruiting events across the Gulf where this disconnect plays out in real time. Students walk in certain their transcript is the differentiator. The recruiter across the table just wants a deliverable, any kind of work sample they can attach to an email with a note that says "look at this." Neither side gets what they came for. The student walks away feeling dismissed for a GPA that took four years to build. The recruiter leaves with a stack of paper and no signal.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How does the skills gap data actually look?</h2>
    <p>NACE (National Association of Colleges and Employers) surveys employers every hiring cycle. Their <a href="https://www.naceweb.org/talent-acquisition/benchmarks/" target="_blank">2024 data shows only 53% of entry-level hires met all stated competency expectations</a> on day one, and the competencies were not exotic: written communication, problem solving, teamwork. What employers got instead was theoretical knowledge that had never been applied to anything resembling real output. Half the new hires showed up knowing the textbook and none of the context.</p>
    <p>BLS numbers tell the same story from the other direction. <a href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/pdf/youth.pdf" target="_blank">Youth unemployment for 20-24 year-olds in early 2025 ran at roughly 7.9%</a>, more than double the <a href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.nr0.htm" target="_blank">national average of roughly 4.1%</a>. These are people who did fine in school, some of them quite well, and they are graduating into a market that has been punishing the credentials-versus-capability mismatch since before they started university. The evaluation system is selecting for something that nobody on the hiring side actually trusts.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What did a real pilot reveal about fixing this?</h2>
    <p>Earlier this year we ran a pilot at a Middle East university that tested something different: students skipped assessments entirely and went straight into scored tasks. Marketing briefs, product requirements, design sprints. Each submission got per-submission feedback on the actual work they produced.</p>
    <p>We got a couple things badly wrong at the start, and the pilot made them obvious fast. We had built a gradual onboarding flow with scaffolded steps and explanations because that is what works in most ed-tech products. Within the first few sessions it was clear that none of it was landing. Students wanted real work inside 90 seconds. They were not curious about the methodology or the scoring system. They wanted to do something tangible and then see if it was any good. Collapsing the introduction to a single "pick your first task" screen immediately increased the number of students who reached their first scored submission, which makes sense in hindsight: we had been asking people to sit through a manual when they came to build.</p>
    <p>The thing that genuinely surprised me was what drove repeat visits. Per-submission feedback turned out to be the entire product. Students told us, without prompting, that they came back specifically to read what someone had written about their work. The generic skill assessments sat untouched. The sidebar with career tips might as well not have existed. We spent weeks trying to figure out why engagement was so lopsided across features, and eventually had to accept that the feedback loop was doing all the heavy lifting while every other module collected dust.</p>
    <p>One student told us she would rather submit something rough and get honest feedback than read another article about how marketing works. Another said he did not realize he could do this kind of work without already holding a job. We heard versions of this repeated across different cohorts, from students whose only common trait was being tired of getting talked at when they wanted to actually produce something.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How do scored work samples change hiring?</h2>
    <p>Schmidt and Hunter's foundational research on personnel selection validity found that <a href="https://www.apa.org/pubs/journals/features/bul-124-2-262.pdf" target="_blank">work-sample tests correlate at about 0.54 with job performance</a>. Sackett et al. confirmed in a <a href="https://psycnet.apa.org/record/2023-38400-001" target="_blank">2022 Personnel Psychology meta-analysis</a> that work samples remain among the top predictors of job performance. Put concretely: work samples predict success about 40% better than unstructured interviews, which come in at 0.38. Both handily beat years of education as a predictor, which sits at 0.10. The research has been pointing in this direction for decades and hiring processes have mostly shrugged at it.</p>
    <p>The reason companies defaulted to interviews anyway is boring and practical: administering work samples at scale was expensive. Someone had to design the test, proctor it, grade it, and that someone was usually a hiring manager who was already underwater. The idea made sense in theory; the logistics made it impossible in practice, so the industry settled for résumés and interviews and learned to live with the noise.</p>
    <p>Simulators change the unit economics. Instead of a recruiter building a one-off evaluation for every open role, students complete calibrated tasks scored against a rubric. Output is assessed automatically. The recruiter gets a sortable signal. The student gets something better than a pass/fail result: a portfolio artifact produced under real conditions, from a real brief.</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Signal quality</th><th>Time invested</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Résumé screen</td><td>Low: self-reported</td><td>Under a minute total</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Phone screen</td><td>Medium: verbal claim</td><td>30 minutes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Assessment test</td><td>High: but generic</td><td>2-3 hours</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scored simulator task</td><td>High: job-identical output</td><td>4-6 hours</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>A simulator task asks more of your time than a résumé screen, and that is the point. You finish with a marketing brief or a product spec or a design file, and recruiters evaluate it the same way they would evaluate work from someone who has been employed for three years because the artifact type is identical. A line on a résumé cannot replicate that. Those hours buy you evidence that compounds across every application and every recruiter search, and it does not expire.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How do you actually become hireable instead of just credentialed?</h2>
    <p>Do real work and put it somewhere public. Do not reach for a class project or a tutorial clone. Pick something that looks like what you would actually do on day one of the job you want. If you want a marketing role, write a campaign brief for a real company. If you want product, write a PRD for an existing product you use daily and know well enough to argue about. Then find someone to score it, collect the feedback, revise, and do it again. Two or three cycles of this will teach you more about what hiring managers actually look for than anything else you can do as a student.</p>
    <p>Chase the kind of feedback that names your specific gaps. Generic encouragement is cheap and priced accordingly. Someone telling you "good effort" does nothing. Someone telling you "your competitive analysis section ignores pricing data and you assumed a market share without checking the S-1" is giving you gold that most people do not get until their first performance review. When I have watched students go through this loop at the pilot, the jump from "I have no idea if this is good" to making revisions that an experienced reviewer would flag took weeks, not months.</p>
    <p>Then make the evidence discoverable. A portfolio is helpful but passive: it sits there waiting for a visitor. What you want is a signal that travels on its own, one that recruiters encounter when they are actively searching for candidates. In a scored platform, recruiters sort by performance percentile and reach out directly. You stop submitting applications into a queue and start having your work speak for you before anyone asks for a résumé.</p>
    <p><a href="https://www.joinzero.ai">zero is built for exactly this</a>. Students complete real tasks for real companies. Their scored output is visible to recruiters. It is free for students.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What makes someone unhireable even with internships?</h2>
    <p>Internships help, but a surprising number of them produce nothing you can actually show anyone later. Photocopying, coffee runs, sitting in on meetings. If that was your day-to-day, the internship exists on your résumé but carries almost no weight, because a recruiter cannot tell from a company name and a title whether you built anything or spent the summer in a room nobody entered. Two candidates with identical internship lines on paper can be worlds apart in capability, and the résumé format is not equipped to expose the difference.</p>
    <p>Graduates who get offers consistently leave every experience with something demonstrable. When I ask hiring managers what separates one junior candidate from another when both have similar internships listed, the answer is never GPA. It is always some variation of "she showed me what she actually built, and I could tell it was real." You want to be the candidate who can say "I built Y feature, it shipped to users, here is the PR link and here is what the metrics did after." That sentence requires evidence, and having the evidence is precisely what makes you credible.</p>
    <p>See how <a href="/learn/how-does-performance-based-hiring-work-for-students">performance-based hiring works for students</a> for the full mechanics.</p>
  </section>

