  <section class="faq-block">
    <h2>What do people ask about graduate hireability?</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-q">Why are fresh graduates considered unhireable by many employers?</div>
      <div class="faq-a">Because they arrive with coursework and credentials but nothing a hiring manager can independently verify. Employers in a <a href="https://www.shrm.org/topics-tools/news/talent-acquisition/skills-gap-college-graduates" target="_blank">2024 SHRM survey reported that 72% of entry-level candidates lack applied skills</a>. The degree itself checks a box. It does not tell anyone whether you can sit down on day one and produce usable work, and that gap between a credential and demonstrated capability is what the 72% number has been measuring across multiple survey cycles now.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-q">Does an internship guarantee you will get hired full-time?</div>
      <div class="faq-a">No. Internships raise your odds but only if you exit with a measurable artifact and a reference who can describe your specific contribution, not just confirm your dates of employment. A lot of internships produce nothing: photocopies, coffee runs, shadowing someone too busy to explain what they are doing. If you cannot point to a single thing you built or shipped during that summer, the line on your résumé registers but barely moves the needle on a recruiter's decision.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-q">How do scored work samples compare to traditional job assessments?</div>
      <div class="faq-a">Work-sample tests predict job performance at a correlation of about 0.54 according to <a href="https://www.apa.org/pubs/journals/features/bul-124-2-262.pdf" target="_blank">Schmidt and Hunter's validity research</a>, confirmed by <a href="https://psycnet.apa.org/record/2023-38400-001" target="_blank">Sackett et al. 2022</a>. That is roughly 40% stronger than unstructured interviews as a predictor. Traditional job assessments are typically multiple-choice tests that measure whether you recognize terminology, not whether you can sit down with a real brief and produce work under realistic constraints.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-q">Can I fix being unhireable without going back to school?</div>
      <div class="faq-a">Yes. Build work samples that are visible to recruiters and demonstrate what you can actually do. A scored simulator task that produces a real deliverable sends a far stronger signal than another credential on your résumé. You can do this alongside your current job or studies on your own schedule, and the whole process can start this week.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-q">How long does it take to build enough proof to become hireable?</div>
      <div class="faq-a">A Middle East university pilot, Spring 2026, showed that students who completed scored tasks with per-submission feedback had recruiter-ready evidence within one academic term. They worked through marketing briefs, product requirements, and design sprints, with each submission receiving specific evaluation of what worked and what needed revision. The improvement came from the tight loop between doing work and hearing back from someone who had actually reviewed it, not from studying more or reading career guides.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-q">What is an outbound signal and why does it matter?</div>
      <div class="faq-a">An outbound signal is evidence of your capability that reaches recruiters without you having to send it anywhere. A résumé is inbound: you apply and wait. A scored work sample on a recruiter-visible platform is outbound: recruiters encounter your percentile ranking during candidate searches and contact you. The direction of the interaction flips from you chasing employers to your work pulling them toward you, and that changes everything about how a job search plays out.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

